(2887174, 404498, 3942, '<td valign="top">I guess you get a lot of comments complaining about the "exactly two objects" in the challenge description. But I think your photo fits the challenge very well, because your main subject are only two objects. Congrats on this funny and technically perfect shot and thanks for sharing! Please write some words about the "how to" into the details. I''m sure the objects are strapped to the tree, but somehow it doesn''t look like that. Very clever ;-)</td>', 'I guess you get a lot of comments complaining about the "exactly two objects" in the challenge description. But I think your photo fits the challenge very well, because your main subject are only two objects. Congrats on this funny and technically perfect shot and thanks for sharing! Please write some words about the "how to" into the details. I''m sure the objects are strapped to the tree, but somehow it doesn''t look like that. Very clever ;-)', '2006-10-02 13:05:08.000000', NULL, 1),
(2887196, 404629, 3942, '<td valign="top">Unrelatedness? Doesn''t has he (or she) teeth? No, no, just kidding, congrats on this funny shot and good luck for the challenge!</td>', 'Unrelatedness? Doesn''t has he (or she) teeth? No, no, just kidding, congrats on this funny shot and good luck for the challenge!', '2006-10-02 13:13:05.000000', NULL, 1),
(2887293, 404697, 33560, '<td valign="top">Or, "time out of a bottle" or "still ticking."   There is something about the the ordered corner, the upright watch and the upright, static goblet that bothers me. On one hand it is the cognitive dissonance, on the other it is the sheer, gratuitous stasis.  I am not smart enough to make a connection between these elements, yet there is nothing to tell me that the whole thing is meant to be bizarre.  I am left wondering "What would the picture have been like if the photographer had, instead, tried to depict pouring time out of a broken glass?"  And so on. Apologies if you find none of this helpful.</td>', 'Or, "time out of a bottle" or "still ticking."   There is something about the the ordered corner, the upright watch and the upright, static goblet that bothers me. On one hand it is the cognitive dissonance, on the other it is the sheer, gratuitous stasis.  I am not smart enough to make a connection between these elements, yet there is nothing to tell me that the whole thing is meant to be bizarre.  I am left wondering "What would the picture have been like if the photographer had, instead, tried to depict pouring time out of a broken glass?"  And so on. Apologies if you find none of this helpful.', '2006-10-02 13:43:26.000000', NULL, 1),
